2. Infrastructure Architecture (Pure Crossplane)
The platform architecture avoids custom operators, utilizing Pure Crossplane to manage the lifecycle of cloud resources, SCM configurations, and GitOps parameters through a hierarchical composition model.
2.1 Crossplane Core Components
The foundation utilizes standard Managed Resources (MRs) to interface with external APIs.
Managed Resource
Function
Provider
Logic for specific APIs (e.g., provider-github, provider-aws).
ProviderConfig
Credentials management; must reference K8s Secrets (spec.credentials.source: Secret).
Repository
Declarative management of GitHub repositories for Application and GitOps code.
EnvironmentConfig
The Data Sharing Layer: Injects org-wide and project-specific settings into Compositions.
Webhook
Configures SCM events to trigger Argo Workflows or GitOps Promoter logic.
2.2 Composite Resource Definitions (XRDs)
Infrastructure is abstracted into high-level, opinionated types:
KubeOrg: The root resource. It initializes the ProviderConfig for GitHub and AWS and creates org-wide EnvironmentConfig objects (e.g., GitHub Org names, AWS Account IDs) that are referenced by all child resources.
KubeProject: Manages environment-level tenancy. It materializes namespaces, project-specific GitOps repositories, and team permissions within the SCM.
KubeApp: The application-level abstraction. It manages source code repositories, links CI/CD templates, and handles the generation of deployment manifests.
2.3 Composition Logic and Wiring
Compositions wire XRDs to Managed Resources using patches and transforms. A critical architectural detail is the use of EnvironmentConfig to maintain idempotency. When a KubeApp claim is created, the Composition selects the relevant EnvironmentConfig via labels (e.g., kubecore.io/kubeorg=primary) to inject repository prefixes and runtime parameters without manual configuration.
